<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=heliocentrism">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heliocentrism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Heliocentric model</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heliocentrism is a superseded astronomical model that placed the Sun at the centre of the universe, with the Earth and the planets orbiting it; the first person known to have proposed it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus of Samos</a>, c. 270 BCE. His writings on it are lost, but his contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a> described the theory in The Sand Reckoner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> turned it from philosophical speculation into predictive geometrical astronomy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-revolutionibus-orbium-coelestium">De revolutionibus</a>, first printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1543, and between 1617 and 1621 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> gave all the planets elliptical orbits around the Sun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heliocentrism.jpg" alt="Heliocentrism" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas Cellarius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heliocentric.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 270 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Greece</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/aristarchus-of-samos">Aristarchus of Samos</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Copernicus revived it as predictive astronomy in 1543; whether Aryabhata’s system of 499 was heliocentric is debated.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy<span>c. 1800 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements<span>c. 300 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Laws of motion and gravitation<span>1687</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=heliocentrism">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
